= Artificial life — Simple =

'''Artificial life''' (ALife) is a field that studies life-like processes by building systems that can be observed and experimented on.

Researchers may build:

* computer simulations;
* self-replicating programs;
* robots;
* artificial ecosystems;
* chemical or protocell systems.

The goal is not always to create a living thing. Often the goal is to understand how processes such as evolution, adaptation, self-organization, or reproduction can arise from simpler mechanisms.

== History ==

The modern field formed around a workshop organized by Christopher Langton in 1987. The proceedings helped establish artificial life as a distinct research community.<ref name="langton">[https://www.routledge.com/Artificial-Life-Proceedings-Of-An-Interdisciplinary-Workshop-On-The-Synthesis/Langton/p/book/9780429032769 Langton, C. G. (ed.) (1989), ''Artificial Life''.]</ref>

A modern review describes ALife as research into “possible life,” extending biological questions beyond only the forms of life already known on Earth.<ref name="dorin">[https://doi.org/10.1162/artl_e_00435 Dorin, A. and Stepney, S. (2024), “What Is Artificial Life Today, and Where Should It Go?”]</ref>

== Three common approaches ==

'''Soft artificial life''' uses software.

Examples include cellular automata, [[Digital organism|digital organisms]], artificial ecosystems, and evolutionary simulations.

'''Hard artificial life''' uses physical machines such as robots.

The machine's body, sensors, motors, energy, and physical environment become part of the experiment.

'''Wet artificial life''' uses chemistry or biological materials.

Researchers may study protocells, membranes, reaction networks, and other systems relevant to the origin or construction of life.

These categories can overlap.

== Digital organisms ==

A digital organism is a program that reproduces inside a computational environment.

Copies can contain mutations. If some variants reproduce more successfully than others, evolution can occur.

Platforms such as Tierra and Avida let researchers record every mutation and lineage.

Lenski and colleagues used Avida to study how a complex function evolved through a sequence of mutations.<ref name="lenski">[https://doi.org/10.1038/nature01568 Lenski, R. E. et al. (2003), “The evolutionary origin of complex features,” ''Nature'' 423, 139–144.]</ref>

This makes digital evolution useful for controlled experiments.

== Cellular automata ==

[[Conway's Game of Life]] is a cellular automaton in which simple local rules produce complicated moving and interacting patterns.

Some patterns can reproduce.

That does not automatically make them biological organisms.

The model is useful because it shows that complex organization can emerge from simple rules.

== Robots ==

Artificial-life robotics studies adaptive behavior in physical bodies.

A robot may use simple local control rules rather than a detailed central plan.

Groups of robots can also show collective behavior such as:

* flocking;
* task allocation;
* exploration;
* collective transport.

Physical robots reveal constraints that software can ignore, including friction, sensor noise, power limits, and mechanical failure.

== Protocells ==

Wet ALife can investigate minimal chemical systems.

A protocell may include:

* a membrane;
* internal reactions;
* energy sources;
* molecules that copy information;
* growth and division.

Researchers ask which combinations are needed for autonomous maintenance and evolution.

[[Autopoiesis]] is one theory that focuses on whether a system produces and maintains the components and boundary that constitute it.

== What counts as life? ==

There is no single definition accepted in every field.

Possible criteria include:

* metabolism;
* self-maintenance;
* reproduction;
* heredity;
* evolution;
* adaptation;
* compartmentalization.

An artificial system may satisfy some criteria and fail others.

For that reason, scientists should describe what a system actually does instead of assuming that the word “life” settles the question.

== Artificial life is not the same as AI ==

[[Artificial intelligence]] usually focuses on capabilities such as prediction, reasoning, planning, language, or control.

Artificial life focuses on processes associated with living organization.

An AI system can be intelligent without reproducing.

A digital organism can reproduce and evolve without being intelligent.

The fields overlap, but they are not the same.

== Open-ended evolution ==

Researchers are interested in systems that continue producing important new adaptations instead of eventually repeating the same kinds of solutions.

This is called '''open-ended evolution'''.

It is difficult to achieve.

A system can keep mutating for a long time without producing genuinely new organization.

Researchers therefore need explicit measures of novelty, diversity, ecological interaction, and adaptation.

== Limits of simulations ==

An artificial-life model is designed by researchers.

The result can depend on:

* the rule set;
* the mutation system;
* the resources available;
* the shape of the environment;
* the fitness or reward function.

A result inside a model does not automatically apply to natural biology.

Scientists must identify which mechanism is general enough to transfer.

== Consciousness ==

A system can evolve, adapt, reproduce, or self-organize without being conscious.

None of those properties by itself proves subjective experience.

Artificial-life research should therefore separate measurable behavior from claims about consciousness.

